.TH CTI-SANITIZE 1 "2026-11-22" "cti-agent 1.7" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-sanitize \- clean text before the orchestrator's model reads it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I command
|
.B cti\-sanitize
.RB [ \-\-source
.IR NAME ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti\-sanitize
reads standard input and writes it to standard output with the characters a
model should not be handed removed. Most of what the orchestrator heartbeat
reads (the journal, the board, the memory database) began in somebody else's
mail or feed, and hidden characters are a way to smuggle instructions past a
human reader.
.PP
It removes control characters other than newline and tab, C1 controls,
bidirectional overrides and zero\-width characters. A run of invalid UTF\-8
becomes one replacement character. A line over 1024 characters is cut and
ends with a
.B [cut N chars]
marker. Output over 64 KB is cut, with a note. Every line it changed is
logged to the journal under the tag
.BR cti\-sanitize .
It writes to standard error only for a usage error, because standard error
goes back into the model.
.PP
The wrappers
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1)
(read and tail) and
.BR fleet\-db (1)
pipe their output through it only inside a heartbeat, that is, when
.B CHECKIN_BEAT_ID
is set by
.BR run\-checkin (1).
Outside a heartbeat their output is untouched, so a lane that parses it is
unaffected. If the cleaner is missing or fails, the wrapper prints nothing:
raw output is not an option inside a beat.
.PP
The rules are the Go package
.IR internal/sanitize ,
which
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR cti\-kev (1),
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)
and
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1)
use to clean their own output inside a beat. It replaced the Python
.BR fleet\-sanitize ,
which kept a second copy of the same rules; the installer removes that file
on update.
.PP
PrimeSec design review 39260, Scenario\-222. The Read grant on
.IR reports/ ,
.I logs/
and
.I archive/
does not pass through the wrappers, so file reads are not cleaned; that gap
is accepted (see SECURITY.md).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-source " NAME"
A label for the journal lines. Only ASCII letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.B .
are kept, at most 40 characters, so a caller cannot forge a log line through
it. Default:
.BR unknown .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHECKIN_BEAT_ID
Not read here. The wrappers use it to decide whether to call this tool.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin/cti\-sanitize
The cleaner, root\-owned. Built by
.BR install\-fedora.sh .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The text was written.
.TP
.B 1
Standard input could not be read or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 2
Arguments other than one
.B \-\-source
.I NAME
were given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Clean a file by hand and see what the journal recorded:
.PP
.EX
/opt/cti\-agent/bin/cti\-sanitize \-\-source test < advisory.txt
sudo journalctl \-t cti\-sanitize \-\-since today
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR checkin\-bash\-guard (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
